1. John wins a one-time $5000 monetary reward on the first day of the year. Add this to his first week's salary:
   Initial salary: 2000 x 1 (week) \boxed{= 2000}
   Add the $5000 reward: 2000 + 5000 \boxed{= 7000}

2. His salary gets a 5% raise for the rest of the year. To find out what his new salary is, multiply his initial salary by 1 + 5% (expressed as a decimal). The decimal for 5% is 0.05:
   New salary: 2000 x (1 + 0.05) \boxed{= 2100 \text{ per week}}

3. There are 52 weeks in a year, so to find out what his total salary for the year is, multiply his new weekly salary by the number of weeks in a year:
   Yearly salary = 2100 x 52 \boxed{= 109200}

4. Account for the one-time $5000 award again (it was part of his initial salary, but it wasn't his yearly salary):
   Total income for the year = 109200 + 5000 \boxed{= 114200}

So, John made \boxed{114,200} in his first year with the award.

